  "pearls": {
   "analysis": "The anchor's mechanics are new to this module, and the difference is the lesson. Case 4's anchor was installed by another clinician before the learner arrived; case 5's was installed by the learner's own true history at stage 1. This one is installed MID-CASE, by a test result: the BNP returns at stage 2 \u2014 a true number, honestly weighed by the workbench itself, which also moves heart failure up (0.12 to 0.2881) \u2014 and the learner converts a rising differential into a settled diagnosis ('the BNP is up, the legs are swollen, it's her heart'), then reads every later result inside the frame: the wide A-a gradient becomes 'fluid', the measured calf becomes 'post-op swelling' (on the un-operated side), the troponin becomes 'demand'. Note what the frame survives: a completed Wells score of 6.0 on the case's own declared block, and a CTPA showing bilateral emboli \u2014 the learner still holds heart failure at 0.16 at stage 3, the stage the workbench rules it out at 0.0041. The counterweights were all mechanical, which is the case's thesis: a score completed from atoms cannot be argued with the way a gestalt can, a tape measure outranks an impression of 'swollen legs', and a peptide that rises in right-sided strain cannot arbitrate between the ventricles. The closing symmetry is deliberate: the test that ends the anchor is the one the learner ordered to confirm it.",
   "description": "Stage 1: a post-operative dyspnoea with a board led, reasonably, by embolism (0.22) over heart failure (0.20) \u2014 the two frames are both alive and the learner's numbers are defensible. Stage 2: the BNP returns at 385 and the learner's board inverts \u2014 heart failure 0.42, embolism down to 0.18 \u2014 while the workbench, applying the same BNP weight, keeps embolism ahead (0.3790 to 0.2881) because it also weighs the D-dimer and cannot un-measure the calf. Stage 3: the CTPA is decisive for the workbench (0.9554) and nearly so for the learner (0.78), but heart failure is still held at 0.16 \u2014 the anchoring event the machinery records, the only one in the attempt. Stage 4: the commit is correct and the residual heart-failure mass (0.05) is below the anchoring line: the frame is released, a stage late and after its own test refuted it.",
   "reactions": "The learner's stage-2 inversion is the most human board in this module so far: a single famous number arriving in a batch of quieter ones. Nothing he ordered was wrong \u2014 the stage-1 panel is the right panel, the CTPA is placed the moment the score completes, and the lung ultrasound is a genuinely good test. The failure is purely interpretive, and it is scored as such.",
   "summary": "Acute dyspnoea twelve days after joint replacement is a vascular presentation until the evidence retires it. Complete the score from atoms before weighing the biomarkers; measure the calves; remember that BNP reports ventricular strain, not ventricular identity; and when your own confirmatory test contradicts your frame, believe the test."
  },
